IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pid-dates`
IMPORT `pid-discloser`

-- Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) -- worked cases for the questions of the person who makes a disclosure.
-- Every expected value is worked by hand from the deposited text (the section is given beside each group), never copied
-- from the encoding's output. Edges: the last day of each period, the day after, month ends and 29 February, each
-- condition failing in turn.
--
-- Sourced from the Western Australian Legislation website, State of Western Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Cases`

`d` MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY

§§ `Dates`

-- Month arithmetic (fork F1): the same day N months on, or the last day of that month.
#ASSERT ((`d` 15 1 2026) `plus months` 3) EQUALS `d` 15 4 2026
#ASSERT ((`d` 30 11 2025) `plus months` 3) EQUALS `d` 28 2 2026
#ASSERT ((`d` 30 11 2027) `plus months` 3) EQUALS `d` 29 2 2028
#ASSERT ((`d` 31 10 2025) `plus months` 3) EQUALS `d` 31 1 2026
#ASSERT ((`d` 31 8 2025) `plus months` 6) EQUALS `d` 28 2 2026
#ASSERT ((`d` 10 12 2025) `plus months` 6) EQUALS `d` 10 6 2026
#ASSERT ((`d` 31 12 2025) `plus months` 12) EQUALS `d` 31 12 2026
#ASSERT ((`d` 1 3 2026) `plus days` 14) EQUALS `d` 15 3 2026
#ASSERT `is a leap year` 2000
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a leap year` 1900))
#ASSERT `the number of days from` (`d` 1 1 2026) `until` (`d` 1 2 2026) EQUALS 31

§§ `s 3 -- public interest information`

`pa` MEANS `a public authority: a department, a State agency or other organisation under the Public Sector Management Act 1994, a local government, a body set up for a public purpose by a written law or by the Governor or a Minister, or a body declared by regulations (s 3)`
`po` MEANS `a public officer: a Minister, a Parliamentary Secretary, a member of Parliament, a judicial officer, a police appointee, a public service officer, a member, officer or employee of a public authority, or another holder of public office (s 3)`
`psc` MEANS `a public sector contractor: a person (not an employee) contracted to supply goods or services to, or perform a public function for, a public authority or the State, or a subcontractor or employee of such a person (s 3)`
`private` MEANS `only a private person or business with no public role and no public contract`
`pii yes` MEANS `yes: it is public interest information (s 3), whether the conduct is past, present or proposed and whenever it happened (s 3, s 5(5)(a))`

#ASSERT `s 3 -- is it public interest information` `pa` TRUE `improper conduct (s 3 public interest information (a))` EQUALS `pii yes`
#ASSERT `s 3 -- is it public interest information` `po` TRUE `an act or omission that is an offence under a written law (s 3 public interest information (b))` EQUALS `pii yes`
#ASSERT `s 3 -- is it public interest information` `psc` TRUE `a substantial unauthorised or irregular use of, or substantial mismanagement of, public resources (s 3 public interest information (c))` EQUALS `pii yes`
#ASSERT `s 3 -- is it public interest information` `pa` TRUE `an act or omission involving a substantial and specific risk of injury to public health, prejudice to public safety or harm to the environment (s 3 public interest information (d))` EQUALS `pii yes`
#ASSERT `s 3 -- is it public interest information` `pa` TRUE `a matter of administration the Ombudsman can investigate under s 14 of the Parliamentary Commissioner Act 1971 (s 3 public interest information (e))` EQUALS `pii yes`
#ASSERT `s 3 -- is it public interest information` `private` TRUE `improper conduct (s 3 public interest information (a))` EQUALS `no: it is not about a public authority, a public officer or a public sector contractor (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 3 -- is it public interest information` `po` FALSE `an act or omission that is an offence under a written law (s 3 public interest information (b))` EQUALS `no: it is not about the performance of a public function (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 3 -- is it public interest information` `psc` TRUE `none of these` EQUALS `no: it does not tend to show any of the kinds of wrongdoing listed (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
-- The first failing condition is reported (private, and no public function, and none of these).
#ASSERT `s 3 -- is it public interest information` `private` FALSE `none of these` EQUALS `no: it is not about a public authority, a public officer or a public sector contractor (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`

§§ `s 5(2) -- appropriate disclosure`

#ASSERT `s 5(2) -- is my disclosure appropriate` `I believe on reasonable grounds that it is true` EQUALS `yes: you believe on reasonable grounds that it is true (s 5(2)(a))`
#ASSERT `s 5(2) -- is my disclosure appropriate` `I have no reasonable grounds to form a belief about whether it is true, but believe on reasonable grounds that it may be true` EQUALS `yes: you believe on reasonable grounds that it may be true (s 5(2)(b))`
#ASSERT `s 5(2) -- is my disclosure appropriate` `neither: I have no reasonable grounds to believe it is or may be true` EQUALS `no: a disclosure is appropriate if, and only if, you believe on reasonable grounds that the information is or may be true (s 5(2), the text's no)`

§§ `s 5(3), (4) -- proper authorities`

-- Argument order: offence, resources, administration, police appointee, judicial officer, MP, another public officer,
-- matter of a public authority, prescribed class.
`police` MEANS `a police officer`
`ccc` MEANS `the Corruption and Crime Commission`
`ag` MEANS `the Auditor General`
`ombudsman` MEANS `the Parliamentary Commissioner for Administrative Investigations (the Ombudsman)`
`pid officer` MEANS `the officer designated under s 23(1)(a) to receive disclosures for the public authority concerned`
`cop` MEANS `the Commissioner of Police`
`cj` MEANS `the Chief Justice`
`presiding` MEANS `the Presiding Officer of the House of Parliament the member belongs to`
`psc commissioner` MEANS `the Public Sector Commissioner`
`declared` MEANS `a person the regulations declare to be a proper authority for information of this class`

-- s 5(3)(a): an offence -- a police officer (the Commissioner of Police among them, F17) or the CCC.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `police`, `ccc`, `cop`
-- (b) public resources -- the Auditor General.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `ag`
-- (c) a matter of administration -- the Ombudsman or the designated officer.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `ombudsman`, `pid officer`
-- (d) a police appointee -- the Commissioner of Police or the CCC.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `ccc`, `cop`
-- (e) a judicial officer -- the Chief Justice.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `cj`
-- (f) a member of Parliament -- the Presiding Officer.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `presiding`
-- (g) another public officer -- the Ombudsman or the Public Sector Commissioner.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `ombudsman`, `psc commissioner`
-- (h) a matter of a public authority -- its designated officer.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS LIST `pid officer`
-- (i) a prescribed class -- the declared person.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS LIST `declared`
-- s 5(4): an offence by a public officer with public money -- (a), (b), (g), (h) together.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS LIST `police`, `ccc`, `ag`, `ombudsman`, `pid officer`, `cop`, `psc commissioner`
-- Nothing in s 5(3) applies: no proper authority.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY

`pa yes` MEANS `yes: a disclosure to this person is made to a proper authority for your information (s 5(3); s 5(4) where more than one paragraph applies)`
`pa no` MEANS `no: this person is not a proper authority for what your information relates to (s 5(3), each paragraph a "where" condition: the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- is this a proper authority for my information` `police` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `pa yes`
-- The Auditor General for an offence alone: not (b).
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- is this a proper authority for my information` `ag` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `pa no`
-- The Public Sector Commissioner for a member of Parliament: (g) excludes members; (f) is the Presiding Officer.
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- is this a proper authority for my information` `psc commissioner` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `pa no`
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- is this a proper authority for my information` `presiding` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `pa yes`
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- is this a proper authority for my information` `a journalist` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: a journalist is not a proper authority; you may go to a journalist only in the cases s 7A(2) sets out, after disclosing to a proper authority`
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- is this a proper authority for my information` `anyone else (a manager, a member of Parliament, a union, the public)` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `pa no`
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- is this a proper authority for my information` `ccc` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `pa yes`
-- The Commissioner of Police for an offence alone: a police officer under (a) (Interpretation Act 1984 s 5; F17).
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- is this a proper authority for my information` `cop` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `pa yes`
#ASSERT `s 5(3) -- is this a proper authority for my information` `declared` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `pa no`

§§ `s 13 -- protection`

-- Argument order: how, public interest information, belief, false or misleading, privileged, anonymous, failed to assist,
-- disclosed elsewhere, relief.
`to pa` MEANS `to a proper authority under s 5`
`true belief` MEANS `I believe on reasonable grounds that it is true`
`no relief` MEANS `no order relieving me`
`protected` MEANS `yes: you incur no civil or criminal liability, and cannot be disciplined, dismissed, have your services terminated, or be held in breach of a duty of secrecy or confidentiality, for making the disclosure (s 13); your liability for your own part in what you disclosed is unaffected (s 6)`
`forfeited no` MEANS `no: you have forfeited the s 13 protection by failing without reasonable excuse to give requested information, or by disclosing the information otherwise than under this Act (s 17(1), the text's no); a court may relieve you if the failure or disclosure did not materially prejudice the public interest and is minor (s 17(2))`

#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `true belief` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `no relief` EQUALS `protected`
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `I have no reasonable grounds to form a belief about whether it is true, but believe on reasonable grounds that it may be true` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `no relief` EQUALS `protected`
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `true belief` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `no relief` EQUALS `protected`
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to someone else` TRUE `true belief` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `no relief` EQUALS `s 13 protects only a disclosure to a proper authority under s 5; this Act gives no protection for a disclosure to anyone else, though other protections are kept (s 25) (NOTHING-kind)`
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` FALSE `true belief` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `no relief` EQUALS `no: it is not public interest information, so s 13 does not reach it (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `true belief` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `no relief` EQUALS `no: a statement made knowing it, or reckless whether it is, false or misleading in a material particular is not protected by this Act (s 24(2), the text's no), and is an offence (s 24(1))`
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `neither: I have no reasonable grounds to believe it is or may be true` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `no relief` EQUALS `no: the disclosure is not an appropriate disclosure, because you do not believe on reasonable grounds that the information is or may be true (s 5(2), the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `true belief` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `no relief` EQUALS `no: nothing in this Act entitles you to disclose information subject to legal professional privilege (s 5(6), the text's no)`
-- s 17(1)(a): failing to assist forfeits; an anonymous informant does not forfeit that way (s 17(2A)).
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `true belief` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `no relief` EQUALS `forfeited no`
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `true belief` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE `no relief` EQUALS `protected`
-- s 17(1)(b) applies to an anonymous informant too.
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `true belief` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE `no relief` EQUALS `forfeited no`
-- s 17(2): relief in whole restores the protection; in part, partly.
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `true belief` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE `an order relieving me in whole` EQUALS `protected`
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `true belief` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `an order relieving me in part` EQUALS `partly: you forfeited the s 13 protection, but a court has relieved you of the forfeiture in part (s 17(2)); the order says how far`
-- A relief order with nothing forfeited changes nothing.
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to pa` TRUE `true belief` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `an order relieving me in part` EQUALS `protected`
-- s 18A and s 13 (fork F4).
#ASSERT `s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `to a journalist, in a case s 7A(2) allows` TRUE `true belief` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `no relief` EQUALS `uncertain: s 18A treats a disclosure to a journalist under s 7A(2) as a disclosure of public interest information for Part 3, but s 13 protects an appropriate disclosure to a proper authority under s 5 (NOTHING-kind; fork F4)`

§§ `s 6`

#ASSERT `s 6 -- does disclosing excuse my own part in it` TRUE EQUALS `no: making the disclosure does not affect your liability for anything the information relates to (s 6, the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 6 -- does disclosing excuse my own part in it` FALSE EQUALS `s 6 does not arise: you had no part in what you disclosed (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `s 10(1) -- notice within 3 months`

#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- the last day to be told what is being done` (`d` 15 1 2026) EQUALS `d` 15 4 2026
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- the last day to be told what is being done` (`d` 30 11 2025) EQUALS `d` 28 2 2026
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- the last day to be told what is being done` (`d` 29 11 2027) EQUALS `d` 29 2 2028
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- the last day to be told what is being done` (`d` 31 10 2025) EQUALS `d` 31 1 2026

-- The regulative rule alone: 90 days allowed (15 January to 15 April 2026).
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- notifying on this day is in time` 90 0
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- notifying on this day is in time` 90 90
#ASSERT (NOT (`s 10(1) -- notifying on this day is in time` 90 91))

-- Argument order: disclosed on, notified, notified on, today, anonymous, CJ or Presiding Officer, another law governs.
`in time` MEANS `told in time: the proper authority notified you within 3 months (s 10(1))`
`s 10 breached` MEANS `the proper authority has not complied with s 10(1): 3 months have passed without notice of the action taken or proposed; you may now go to a journalist (s 7A(2)(d))`
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` (`d` 15 1 2026) TRUE (`d` 15 4 2026) (`d` 1 5 2026) FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `in time`
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` (`d` 15 1 2026) TRUE (`d` 14 4 2026) (`d` 1 5 2026) FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `in time`
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` (`d` 15 1 2026) TRUE (`d` 15 1 2026) (`d` 1 5 2026) FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `in time`
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` (`d` 15 1 2026) TRUE (`d` 16 4 2026) (`d` 1 5 2026) FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 10 breached`
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` (`d` 15 1 2026) FALSE (`d` 1 1 2000) (`d` 15 4 2026) FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not yet due: 3 months since your disclosure have not passed (s 10(1))`
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` (`d` 15 1 2026) FALSE (`d` 1 1 2000) (`d` 16 4 2026) FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 10 breached`
-- 30 November 2025: the last day is 28 February 2026 (fork F1).
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` (`d` 30 11 2025) TRUE (`d` 28 2 2026) (`d` 1 5 2026) FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `in time`
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` (`d` 30 11 2025) TRUE (`d` 1 3 2026) (`d` 1 5 2026) FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 10 breached`
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` (`d` 15 1 2026) FALSE (`d` 1 1 2000) (`d` 16 4 2026) TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 10 does not apply: you disclosed anonymously (s 10(5), the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` (`d` 15 1 2026) FALSE (`d` 1 1 2000) (`d` 16 4 2026) FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 10 does not apply: the Chief Justice and the Presiding Officers are not proper authorities for ss 8 to 10 (s 7, the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` (`d` 15 1 2026) FALSE (`d` 1 1 2000) (`d` 16 4 2026) FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 10 does not apply to this authority: it deals with the matter, or reports progress, under another law (s 12(1), (4), the text's no)`

§§ `s 10(2)-(4) -- reports`

-- Argument order: anonymous, asked, complete, declared body with its own reporting duty.
`final report` MEANS `a final report: as the investigation is complete, the proper authority that carried it out must give you a report of the outcome, any action taken or proposed, and the reasons (s 10(4)); it must leave out anything likely to affect a person's safety, an investigation of an offence, or another discloser's confidentiality (s 11)`
#ASSERT `s 10 -- what report can I get` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `final report`
#ASSERT `s 10 -- what report can I get` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `a progress report, at the authority's discretion: the investigation is not complete, and the authority may, but need not, report on its current status (s 10(3))`
#ASSERT `s 10 -- what report can I get` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `ask first: you may ask the proper authority for a report on progress (s 10(2)); once asked, it must give a final report when the investigation is complete (s 10(4))`
#ASSERT `s 10 -- what report can I get` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `none under this Act: s 10(2) to (4) do not apply to an anonymous disclosure (s 10(5), the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 10 -- what report can I get` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `a progress report as if your disclosure were a complaint under the law that governs this authority (s 12(4)(b)); s 10 does not apply to it (s 12(4)(a))`
-- s 12(5A): no s 12(4)(b) report for an anonymous informant either.
#ASSERT `s 10 -- what report can I get` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `none under this Act: s 10(2) to (4) do not apply to an anonymous disclosure (s 10(5), the text's no)`
-- s 7: a disclosure to the Chief Justice or a Presiding Officer brings no s 10 report, even when asked and complete.
#ASSERT `s 10 -- what report can I get` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `none under this Act: the Chief Justice and the Presiding Officers are not proper authorities for ss 8 to 10 (s 7, the text's no)`
-- s 12(1): the CCC or the Ombudsman dealing with the matter under its own law need not give an s 10 report.
#ASSERT `s 10 -- what report can I get` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `none required by this Act: the Corruption and Crime Commission and the Ombudsman need not comply with s 10 for a matter they deal with under another law (s 12(1), the text's no); that law may give you a report`
#ASSERT `s 10 -- what report can I get` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `none required by this Act: the Corruption and Crime Commission and the Ombudsman need not comply with s 10 for a matter they deal with under another law (s 12(1), the text's no); that law may give you a report`

§§ `s 8 -- investigation`

-- Argument order: CJ or Presiding Officer, relates to the authority, another law governs, ground, anonymous.
`no ground` MEANS `none: the authority does not consider any ground in s 8(2) applies`
`must investigate` MEANS `yes: the proper authority must investigate it or cause it to be investigated (s 8(1))`
`may refuse, reasons` MEANS `the authority may refuse to investigate, or stop investigating, on the ground it considers applies (s 8(2)); it must tell you its reason (s 8(3))`
#ASSERT `s 8 -- must my disclosure be investigated` FALSE TRUE FALSE `no ground` FALSE EQUALS `must investigate`
#ASSERT `s 8 -- must my disclosure be investigated` FALSE TRUE FALSE `the matter is trivial (s 8(2)(a))` FALSE EQUALS `may refuse, reasons`
#ASSERT `s 8 -- must my disclosure be investigated` FALSE TRUE FALSE `the disclosure is vexatious or frivolous (s 8(2)(b))` FALSE EQUALS `may refuse, reasons`
#ASSERT `s 8 -- must my disclosure be investigated` FALSE TRUE FALSE `no reasonable prospect of sufficient evidence, because of the time since the matter happened (s 8(2)(c))` FALSE EQUALS `may refuse, reasons`
#ASSERT `s 8 -- must my disclosure be investigated` FALSE TRUE FALSE `the matter is being or has been adequately investigated by another proper authority (s 8(2)(d))` FALSE EQUALS `may refuse, reasons`
#ASSERT `s 8 -- must my disclosure be investigated` FALSE TRUE FALSE `the matter is trivial (s 8(2)(a))` TRUE EQUALS `the authority may refuse to investigate, or stop investigating, on the ground it considers applies (s 8(2)); as you disclosed anonymously it need not tell you why (s 8(4))`
#ASSERT `s 8 -- must my disclosure be investigated` TRUE TRUE FALSE `no ground` FALSE EQUALS `not under this Act: the Chief Justice and the Presiding Officers are not proper authorities for ss 8 to 10 (s 7, the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 8 -- must my disclosure be investigated` FALSE TRUE TRUE `no ground` FALSE EQUALS `not under this Act: the authority deals with the matter under its own law, and s 8(1) does not bind it (s 12(1), (3), the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 8 -- must my disclosure be investigated` FALSE FALSE FALSE `no ground` FALSE EQUALS `s 8(1) does not require this authority to investigate: the disclosure does not relate to it, its officers or contractors, or a matter it has a function or power to investigate (NOTHING-kind; fork F6)`
-- s 12 lifts s 8(1) only; a refusal still carries the s 8(3) duty to give reasons.
#ASSERT `s 8 -- must my disclosure be investigated` FALSE TRUE TRUE `the matter is trivial (s 8(2)(a))` FALSE EQUALS `may refuse, reasons`

§§ `s 7A -- a journalist`

#ASSERT `s 7A(2)(b) -- the last day of the 6 months` (`d` 15 1 2026) EQUALS `d` 15 7 2026
#ASSERT `s 7A(2)(b) -- the last day of the 6 months` (`d` 31 8 2025) EQUALS `d` 28 2 2026
#ASSERT `s 7A(2)(b) -- the last day of the 6 months` (`d` 31 8 2027) EQUALS `d` 29 2 2028

-- Argument order: already disclosed, disclosed on, today, refused or discontinued, completed, completed on, action
-- recommended, anonymous, s 10 applies, notified, notified on, asked for a report, final report given.
-- Base: disclosed 15 January 2026, notified in time on 1 February 2026, s 10 applies.
`j none` MEANS `no: none of the cases in s 7A(2)(a) to (d) has arisen (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
`j b` MEANS `yes: the investigation was not completed within 6 months after your disclosure (s 7A(2)(b))`
`j d` MEANS `yes: the proper authority has not complied with s 10(1) (notice within 3 months) or s 10(4) (a final report you asked for) (s 7A(2)(d))`
`jan15` MEANS `d` 15 1 2026
`feb1` MEANS `d` 1 2 2026
`unused` MEANS `d` 1 1 2000

#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` FALSE `jan15` (`d` 1 8 2026) TRUE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `feb1` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 7A reaches only substantially the same information you have already disclosed under this Act to a proper authority (NOTHING-kind)`
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 1 3 2026) TRUE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `feb1` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the proper authority refused to investigate, or discontinued the investigation (s 7A(2)(a))`
-- (b): open on 15 July 2026 the last day, not yet; on 16 July, yes.
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 15 7 2026) FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `feb1` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `j none`
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 16 7 2026) FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `feb1` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `j b`
-- (b): completed on 16 July (late) with action recommended; on 15 July, in time.
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 1 8 2026) FALSE TRUE (`d` 16 7 2026) TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE `feb1` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `j b`
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 1 8 2026) FALSE TRUE (`d` 15 7 2026) TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE `feb1` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `j none`
-- (c): completed in time, no action recommended.
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 1 8 2026) FALSE TRUE (`d` 1 6 2026) FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `feb1` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the investigation was completed but no action was recommended (s 7A(2)(c))`
-- (d) by s 10(1): not notified, today 16 April 2026 (after the 3 months, inside the 6).
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 16 4 2026) FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `j d`
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 15 4 2026) FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `j none`
-- (d) not applicable: anonymous (s 10(5)), or s 10 not applying to the authority.
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 16 4 2026) FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `j none`
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 16 4 2026) FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `j none`
-- (d) by s 10(4): complete with action recommended, report asked for and not given (fork F5); given: no.
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 1 4 2026) FALSE TRUE (`d` 1 3 2026) TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE `feb1` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `j d`
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 1 4 2026) FALSE TRUE (`d` 1 3 2026) TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE `feb1` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `j none`
-- A disclosure to the Chief Justice or a Presiding Officer (s 10 does not apply, no s 8 duty): (b) still opens after 6 months (finding 6).
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 16 7 2026) FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `j b`
-- Notified late (16 April) counts under (d) even after notice.
#ASSERT `s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` TRUE `jan15` (`d` 1 5 2026) FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE (`d` 16 4 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS `j d`

§§ `s 17(1)(a) -- helping the investigation`

#ASSERT `s 17(1)(a) -- supplying on this day is in time` 14 14
#ASSERT (NOT (`s 17(1)(a) -- supplying on this day is in time` 14 15))

-- Argument order: asked on, days specified, supplied, supplied on, today, reasonable excuse, anonymous.
`mar1` MEANS `d` 1 3 2026
`forfeit` MEANS `you forfeit the s 13 protection: the information was not supplied within the period specified, without reasonable excuse (s 17(1)(a), the text's no); a court may relieve you if the failure did not materially prejudice the public interest and is minor (s 17(2))`
#ASSERT `s 17(1)(a) -- did I help the investigation in time` `mar1` 14 TRUE (`d` 15 3 2026) (`d` 20 3 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS `in time: you supplied the information within the period specified (s 17(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `s 17(1)(a) -- did I help the investigation in time` `mar1` 14 TRUE (`d` 16 3 2026) (`d` 20 3 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS `forfeit`
#ASSERT `s 17(1)(a) -- did I help the investigation in time` `mar1` 14 TRUE (`d` 16 3 2026) (`d` 20 3 2026) TRUE FALSE EQUALS `you supplied it late or not at all, but with a reasonable excuse, so you do not forfeit your protection (s 17(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `s 17(1)(a) -- did I help the investigation in time` `mar1` 14 FALSE `unused` (`d` 15 3 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not yet due: the period specified has not ended (s 17(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `s 17(1)(a) -- did I help the investigation in time` `mar1` 14 FALSE `unused` (`d` 16 3 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS `forfeit`
#ASSERT `s 17(1)(a) -- did I help the investigation in time` `mar1` 14 FALSE `unused` (`d` 16 3 2026) FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 17(1)(a) does not apply: you disclosed anonymously (s 17(2A), the text's no)`
-- Across a month end: asked 20 February 2026, 14 days: the last day is 6 March 2026.
#ASSERT `s 17(1)(a) -- did I help the investigation in time` (`d` 20 2 2026) 14 TRUE (`d` 6 3 2026) (`d` 10 3 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS `in time: you supplied the information within the period specified (s 17(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `s 17(1)(a) -- did I help the investigation in time` (`d` 20 2 2026) 14 TRUE (`d` 7 3 2026) (`d` 10 3 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS `forfeit`

§§ `s 15 -- victimisation`

-- Argument order: what, why, on, against, route, already took the other route, employer proves defence.
`harassed` MEANS `intimidation or harassment (s 3 detrimental action (b))`
`because` MEANS `because, or substantially because, I or someone else made, or intends to make, a disclosure of public interest information under this Act`
`perp` MEANS `the person who did or threatened it (the perpetrator)`
`employer` MEANS `the perpetrator's employer`
`tort` MEANS `proceedings in tort in a court (s 15(1))`
`eo` MEANS `a complaint under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (s 15(4))`
`sue perp` MEANS `yes: it is an act of victimisation, which you may sue for as a tort against the perpetrator (s 15(1), (2))`

#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `harassed` `because` (`d` 1 6 2026) `perp` `tort` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `sue perp`
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `injury, damage or loss (s 3 detrimental action (a))` `because` (`d` 1 6 2026) `perp` `tort` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `sue perp`
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `adverse discrimination, disadvantage or adverse treatment in my career, profession, employment, trade or business (s 3 detrimental action (c))` `because` (`d` 1 6 2026) `perp` `tort` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `sue perp`
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `a reprisal (s 3 detrimental action (d))` `because` (`d` 1 6 2026) `perp` `tort` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `sue perp`
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `harassed` `because` (`d` 1 6 2026) `employer` `tort` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is an act of victimisation, which you may sue for as a tort against the perpetrator's employer; the employer has a defence if it proves it was not knowingly involved, did not and could not reasonably have known, and could not by reasonable care have prevented it (s 15(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `harassed` `because` (`d` 1 6 2026) `employer` `tort` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the employer has proved the defence in s 15(3) (not knowingly involved, did not and could not reasonably have known, could not by reasonable care have prevented it), so s 15 gives no claim against the employer on these facts (the text's no)`
-- The employer's defence does not touch a claim against the perpetrator.
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `harassed` `because` (`d` 1 6 2026) `perp` `tort` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `sue perp`
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `harassed` `because` (`d` 1 6 2026) `perp` `eo` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you may complain under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 as if it were an act unlawful under s 67 of that Act (s 15(4)); the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity may decline it if it has already been adequately dealt with by a competent authority (s 15(5))`
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `harassed` `because` (`d` 1 6 2026) `perp` `tort` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: having lodged a complaint under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 about this act, you cannot then sue in tort for it (s 15(4), the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `harassed` `because` (`d` 1 6 2026) `perp` `eo` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: having commenced proceedings in a court about this act, you cannot then complain under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (s 15(4), the text's no)`
-- s 15(6): commencement 1 July 2003; 30 June 2003 is before it.
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `harassed` `because` (`d` 30 6 2003) `perp` `tort` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 15 has no retrospective operation, and no proceeding may be taken for an act before the Act commenced on 1 July 2003 (s 15(6), the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `harassed` `because` (`d` 1 7 2003) `perp` `tort` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `sue perp`
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `harassed` `for some other reason` (`d` 1 6 2026) `perp` `tort` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: it is an act of victimisation only if done because, or substantially because, of a disclosure under this Act (s 15(1), the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `some other harm or disadvantage not listed` `because` (`d` 1 6 2026) `perp` `tort` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `perhaps: the harm is not one of the kinds listed in the definition of detrimental action, which "includes" them without closing the list (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `s 15A -- injunction`

#ASSERT `s 15A -- can I get an injunction` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `you may apply: the Supreme Court may make an interim order or grant an interim injunction until it decides (s 15A(1), (3)); a final order needs the Court to be satisfied the action was taken or intended in reprisal (s 15A(2))`
#ASSERT `s 15A -- can I get an injunction` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court, being satisfied, may order the person who took the detrimental action to remedy it, or grant an injunction in any terms it considers appropriate (s 15A(2))`
#ASSERT `s 15A -- can I get an injunction` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no order under s 15A(2): the Court is not satisfied that detrimental action was taken or intended in reprisal for a disclosure (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
#ASSERT `s 15A -- can I get an injunction` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 15A is for a person who believes detrimental action has been or may be taken against them in reprisal for a disclosure under this Act (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `s 15B -- relocation`

-- Argument order: employment, reprisal, only practical means, applied in writing, authority satisfied, I consent.
`pso` MEANS `a public service officer`
`relocate yes` MEANS `yes: your employing authority must, as far as practicable, arrange to relocate you away from your existing work location, within your department or organisation or to another, but only with your consent to the specific arrangements (s 15B(3), (4))`
#ASSERT `s 15B -- can I be relocated` `pso` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `relocate yes`
#ASSERT `s 15B -- can I be relocated` `an employee employed in an organisation under the Public Sector Management Act 1994` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `relocate yes`
#ASSERT `s 15B -- can I be relocated` `something else (a contractor, a local government employee, a private employee)` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 15B is for public service employees only; other employees are not covered by it (NOTHING-kind); the principal executive officer of a public authority must protect its employees who disclose from detrimental action (s 23(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `s 15B -- can I be relocated` `pso` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 15B(2) lets you apply only on both grounds: detrimental action in reprisal, and relocation the only practical means of removing the danger (NOTHING-kind)`
#ASSERT `s 15B -- can I be relocated` `pso` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 15B(2) lets you apply only on both grounds: detrimental action in reprisal, and relocation the only practical means of removing the danger (NOTHING-kind)`
#ASSERT `s 15B -- can I be relocated` `pso` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `you may apply: apply in writing to your employing authority on the grounds in s 15B(2); it must arrange your relocation if satisfied the grounds are established (s 15B(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `s 15B -- can I be relocated` `pso` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no duty to relocate: the employing authority is not satisfied that the grounds are established (s 15B(3), the text's no: its "if" fails)`
#ASSERT `s 15B -- can I be relocated` `pso` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the arrangements need your consent: the employing authority may not relocate you without your consent to the specific arrangements it proposes (s 15B(4), the text's no)`

§§ `s 16(1), (2) -- my identity`

-- Argument order: appropriate disclosure, I consent, natural justice, investigate, court order, CCM Act, anonymous,
-- advised beforehand.
#ASSERT `s 16(1) -- can my identity be revealed` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: no one may reveal information that might identify you as the person who made the disclosure; doing so is an offence, up to $24,000 or 2 years' imprisonment (s 16(1), the text's no)`
#ASSERT `s 16(1) -- can my identity be revealed` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you consented (s 16(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `s 16(1) -- can my identity be revealed` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is made in accordance with an order of a court or other body with authority to hear evidence (s 16(1)(d))`
#ASSERT `s 16(1) -- can my identity be revealed` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is made in accordance with s 152 or 153 of the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act 2003 (s 16(1)(f))`
#ASSERT `s 16(1) -- can my identity be revealed` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: it is necessary for natural justice or to investigate the matter effectively, and you were advised beforehand of the disclosure and the reason (s 16(1)(b), (c), (2))`
#ASSERT `s 16(1) -- can my identity be revealed` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, on the ground of natural justice or effective investigation (s 16(1)(b), (c)), but the person must first take all reasonable steps to tell you, a reasonable time before, that it will be made and why (s 16(2)), and has not`
#ASSERT `s 16(1) -- can my identity be revealed` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is necessary for natural justice or to investigate the matter effectively (s 16(1)(b), (c)); as you disclosed anonymously, no advance notice is required (s 16(3A))`
#ASSERT `s 16(1) -- can my identity be revealed` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 16(1) protects the identity of a person who made an appropriate disclosure; it does not reach this one (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `s 16(3) -- the person the disclosure is about`

`may not reveal` MEANS `no: you must not reveal information that might identify the person your disclosure is about; doing so is an offence, up to $24,000 or 2 years' imprisonment (s 16(3), the text's no); and disclosing the contents of your disclosure otherwise than under this Act forfeits your s 13 protection (s 17(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `s 16(3) -- may I reveal who my disclosure is about` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `may not reveal`
#ASSERT `s 16(3) -- may I reveal who my disclosure is about` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the person your disclosure is about consents (s 16(3)(a))`
#ASSERT `s 16(3) -- may I reveal who my disclosure is about` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is necessary to enable the matter to be investigated effectively (s 16(3)(b))`
#ASSERT `s 16(3) -- may I reveal who my disclosure is about` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: there are reasonable grounds to believe it is necessary to prevent or minimise the risk of injury to a person or damage to property (s 16(3)(d))`
#ASSERT `s 16(3) -- may I reveal who my disclosure is about` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is in accordance with an order of a court or other body with authority to hear evidence (s 16(3)(e))`
#ASSERT `s 16(3) -- may I reveal who my disclosure is about` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: it is in accordance with s 152 or 153 of the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act 2003 (s 16(3)(g))`

§§ `s 24 -- false or misleading disclosure`

`s 24 offence` MEANS `yes: a statement to a proper authority purporting to be a disclosure, made knowing or reckless that it is false or misleading in a material particular, is an offence, up to $12,000 or 1 year's imprisonment, and is not protected by this Act (s 24(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `s 24 -- is my disclosure an offence` `I know it is false or misleading in a material particular` TRUE EQUALS `s 24 offence`
#ASSERT `s 24 -- is my disclosure an offence` `I am reckless about whether it is false or misleading in a material particular` TRUE EQUALS `s 24 offence`
#ASSERT `s 24 -- is my disclosure an offence` `I believe it is true, or may be true` TRUE EQUALS `s 24 does not make it an offence: you are neither knowing nor reckless about its being false or misleading (NOTHING-kind)`
#ASSERT `s 24 -- is my disclosure an offence` `I know it is false or misleading in a material particular` FALSE EQUALS `s 24 does not reach a statement that is not made to a proper authority (s 24(1a)) (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `ss 14, 16, 24 -- penalties`

#ASSERT (`ss 14, 16, 24 -- the most a person can be punished` `taking or threatening detrimental action against someone because of a disclosure (s 14(1))`)'s `the most in dollars` EQUALS 24000
#ASSERT (`ss 14, 16, 24 -- the most a person can be punished` `taking or threatening detrimental action against someone because of a disclosure (s 14(1))`)'s `the most years in prison` EQUALS 2
#ASSERT (`ss 14, 16, 24 -- the most a person can be punished` `attempting, or inciting another, to take detrimental action because of a disclosure (s 14(2))`)'s `the most in dollars` EQUALS 24000
#ASSERT (`ss 14, 16, 24 -- the most a person can be punished` `revealing the identity of a person who made a disclosure (s 16(1))`)'s `the most in dollars` EQUALS 24000
#ASSERT (`ss 14, 16, 24 -- the most a person can be punished` `revealing the identity of the person a disclosure is about (s 16(3))`)'s `the most years in prison` EQUALS 2
#ASSERT (`ss 14, 16, 24 -- the most a person can be punished` `making a false or misleading disclosure knowingly or recklessly (s 24(1))`)'s `the most in dollars` EQUALS 12000
#ASSERT (`ss 14, 16, 24 -- the most a person can be punished` `making a false or misleading disclosure knowingly or recklessly (s 24(1))`)'s `the most years in prison` EQUALS 1
